            "  | B     |  1  |  0  |  0  |  1  |  0  |",
            "  | C     |  0  |  0  |  0  |  0  |  0  |",
            "  | D     |  1  |  1  |  0  |  0  |  1  |",
            "  | E     |  0  |  0  |  0  |  1  |  0  |",
            "",
            "  <!-- data-title=\"Directed Graph\" -->",
            "  | Graph |  A  |  B  |  C  |  D  |  E  |",
            "  |:----- |:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|",
            "  | A     |  0  | 12  |  0  |  1  |  0  |",
            "  | B     | -22 |  0  |  0  | 0.4 |  0  |",
            "  | C     |  0  |  0  |  0  |  0  |  0  |",
            "  | D     |  2  | 12  |  0  |  0  |  1  |",
            "  | E     |  0  |  0  |  0  |  2  |  0  |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-graph"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"heatmap\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"heatmap\" -->",
        "description": [
            "Another type of visualization is a HeatMap, which is used, if the table head and the first column do only contain numbers, in other words coordinates. If you want to use categories instead of coordinate numbers, you can enforce the usage of a heatmap, with the comment shown below:",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!--",
            "  data-type=\"heatmap\"",
            "  data-title=\"Seattle mean temperature in Fahrenheit\"",
            "  data-show",
            "  -->",
            "    | Seattle |  Jan |  Feb |  Mar |  Apr |  May |  ... |",
            "    | -------:| ----:| ----:| ----:| ----:| ----:| ----:|",
            "    |       0 | 40.7 | 41.5 | 43.6 | 46.6 | 51.4 |  ... |",
            "    |       2 |  ... |  ... |  ... |  ... |  ... |  ... |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-heatmap"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"lineplot\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"lineplot\" -->",
        "description": [
            "If the first column contains (not repeating) numbers as well as the other columns, lineplots are used as the default representation, a special tag is not required. If this isn't the case, then you can apply this tag to enforce the visualization as a lineplot.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"lineplot\" -->",
            "  | x's |  some y's  |    dist |",
            "  | --- |:----------:| -------:|",
            "  | 1   |    1 $    | 16 $km$ |",
            "  | 2.2 |    2 $    | 12 $km$ |",
            "  | 3.3 |    5 $    |  1 $km$ |",
            "  | 4   | -12.333 $ |         |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-lineplot"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"map\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"map\" -->",
        "description": [
            "A map is similar to a BarChart from the table structure, but if you want to depict your data on a real map, you will have to add a geojson-file, that contains all relevant data about the form of your countries, states, cities, etc. The first column has to match the names of your objects in your geojson data, that is attached to your table in the following way:",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"map\" data-src=\"https://code.highcharts.com/mapdata/custom/europe.geo.json\" -->",
            "  | Country                | percent |",
            "  | ---------------------- | ------- |",
            "  | Albania                | 73.5    |",
            "  | Andorra                | 98.9    |",
            "  | Armenia                | 72.4    |",
            "  | ...                    |         |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-map"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"none\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"none\" -->",
        "description": [
            "Simply data-type=\"none\" to prevent any kind of visualization.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"none\" -->",
            "  | Sankey |  A  |  B  |  C  |  D  |  E  |",
            "  |:------ |:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|",
            "  | A      |     |  2  |     |     |     |",
            "  | B      |  3  |     |     |     |     |",
            "  | C      |  1  |  1  |     |     |     |",
            "  | D      |     |  1  |  1  |     |     |",
            "  | E      |  2  |  1  |  1  |  1  |     |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-none"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"parallel\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"parallel\" -->",
        "description": [
            "A parallel coordinates plot draws one vertical axis per column and one line per row. It is useful if there are too many categories for a readable BarChart. Do not rely on the automatic detection, set the type explicitly.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"parallel\" -->",
            "  | Country                | GDP growth (%) | Births per woman | Life expectancy ...",
            "  | ---------------------- | --------------:| ----------------:| -------------------",
            "  | Albania                |            7.5 |            1.858 |               76.63",
            "  | Andorra                |          3.570 |            1.260 |                 NaN",
            "  | Austria                |          2.178 |            1.414 |               80.44",
            "  | ...                    |            ... |              ... |                 ..."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-parallel"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"piechart\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"piechart\" -->",
        "description": [
            "If you have a table with only one row full of numbers, this will be automatically presented as an pie chart. The head represents the categories and the body the quatities.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  | Classic | Country | Reggae | Hip-Hop | Hard-Rock | Samba |",
            "  | -------:| -------:| ------:| -------:| ---------:| -----:|",
            "  |      50 |      50 |    100 |     200 |       350 |   250 |",
            "",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"PieChart\" -->",
            "  | Music-Style | Classic | Country | Reggae | Hip-Hop | Hard-Rock | Samba |",
            "  |:----------- | -------:| -------:| ------:| -------:| ---------:| -----:|",
            "  | 1994        |      50 |      50 |    100 |     200 |       350 |   250 |",
            "  | 2014        |      20 |      30 |    100 |     220 |       400 |   230 |",
            "  | demo 2034   |       5 |      12 |     98 |     293 |       345 |    32 |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-piechart"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"radar\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"radar\" -->",
        "description": [
            "A radar chart draws one polygon per row with one axis per column. It is useful if the columns have very different scales (e.g. weight in kg and lifespan in years), which would make a BarChart unreadable. Do not rely on the automatic detection, set the type explicitly. Use at least 3 numeric columns.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"radar\" -->",
            "  | Animal          | weight in kg | Lifespan years | Mitogen |",
            "  | --------------- | ------------:| --------------:| -------:|",
            "  | Mouse           |        0.028 |              2 |      95 |",
            "  | Flying squirrel |        0.085 |             15 |      50 |",
            "  | Brown bat       |        0.020 |             30 |      10 |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-radar"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"sankey\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"sankey\" -->",
        "description": [
            "A Sankey diagram is a special type of directed graph that can be used to streams or the flow of something, such as engergy, money, etc.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"sankey\" -->",
            "  | Sankey |  A  |  B  |  C  |  D  |  E  |",
            "  |:------ |:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|",
            "  | A      |     |  2  |     |     |     |",
            "  | B      |  3  |     |     |     |     |",
            "  | C      |  1  |  1  |     |     |     |",
            "  | D      |     |  1  |  1  |     |     |",
            "  | E      |  2  |  1  |  1  |  1  |     |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-sankey"
    },
    "<!-- data-type=\"scatterplot\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-type=\"scatterplot\" -->",
        "description": [
            "If the first column contains repetitive numbers, scatterplots are used as the default representation if further numbers are determine, a special tag is not required.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"scatterplot\" -->",
            "  | x's |  some y's  |                              dist |",
            "  | --- |:----------:| ---------------------------------:|",
            "  | 1   |    1 $    |                           16 $km$ |",
            "  | 2.2 |    2 $    |                           12 $km$ |",
            "  | 3.3 |    5 $    |                            1 $km$ |",
            "  | 4   | -12.333 $ | -555$km$ <-- this will be ignored |",
            "  | 4   |            |                                 1 |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-scatterplot"
    },
    "<!-- data-xlabel=\"...\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-xlabel=\"${1:title}\" -->",
        "description": [
            "You can overwrite the diagram x-label for your plotted document via the data-xlabel parameter, this is only visible if you switch to the diagram mode.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!--",
            "  data-title=\"Government expenditure on education\"",
            "  data-xlabel=\"year\"",
            "  data-ylabel=\"% of GDP\"",
            "  -->",
            "  | Year | Finland | USA | Germany |   China |",
            "  | ---- | -------:| ---:| -------:| -------:|",
            "  | 1995 | 6.80942 |     | 4.42079 | 1.84192 |",
            "  | 1996 | 6.86052 |     | 4.48319 | 1.85338 |",
            "  | ...  |     ... | ... |     ... |     ... |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-xlabel"
    },
    "<!-- data-xlim=\"min,max\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-xlim=\"${1:0},${2:100}\" -->",
        "description": [
            "Sets the range of the x-axis. One side can be left empty to determine it automatically, e.g. data-xlim=\",12\".",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-show data-xlim=\"1994,2000\" -->",
            "  | Year | Finland | Germany |",
            "  | ---- | -------:| -------:|",
            "  | 1995 |     6.8 |     4.4 |",
            "  | 1996 |     6.9 |     4.5 |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-xlim"
    },
    "<!-- data-ylabel=\"...\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-ylabel=\"${1:title}\" -->",
        "description": [
            "You can overwrite the diagram y-label for your plotted document via the data-ylabel parameter, this is only visible if you switch to the diagram mode.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!--",
            "  data-title=\"Government expenditure on education\"",
            "  data-xlabel=\"year\"",
            "  data-ylabel=\"% of GDP\"",
            "  -->",
            "  | Year | Finland | USA | Germany |   China |",
            "  | ---- | -------:| ---:| -------:| -------:|",
            "  | 1995 | 6.80942 |     | 4.42079 | 1.84192 |",
            "  | 1996 | 6.86052 |     | 4.48319 | 1.85338 |",
            "  | ...  |     ... | ... |     ... |     ... |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-ylabel"
    },
    "<!-- data-ylim=\"min,max\" -->": {
        "body": "<!-- data-ylim=\"${1:0},${2:100}\" -->",
        "description": [
            "Sets the range of the y-axis. One side can be left empty to determine it automatically, e.g. data-ylim=\"0,\".",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- data-show data-ylim=\"0,10\" -->",
            "  | Year | Finland | Germany |",
            "  | ---- | -------:| -------:|",
            "  | 1995 |     6.8 |     4.4 |",
            "  | 1996 |     6.9 |     4.5 |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-table-diagram-ylim"
    },
    "<!-- name: ...": {
        "body": "<!--\nauthor:   ${1:Your Name}\nemail:    ${2:your@email.com}\nversion:  ${3:0.1.0}\nlanguage: ${4:en}\nnarrator: ${5:US English Female}\n\ncomment:  ${6:A short description of your course.\n          Multiline is also okay.}\n\n${7:logo:     ./img/logo.png}\n-->",
        "description": [
            "The main header is an HTML comment at the very top of the document. It defines the defaults of the course: authors, contact, version, language, the narrator voice, additional JavaScript and CSS, macros and more. Continuation lines have to be indented. Most settings can be overwritten per section (see lia-header-section).",
            "",
            "Common fields:",
            "  author      separate multiple authors with ;",
            "  email       contact shown in the info panel",
            "  version     0.x.x = development, nothing is stored;",
            "              >= 1.0.0 = quiz, code and task states are stored",
            "              (bump the major version when you move quizzes)",
            "  language    UI language and typography (en, de, fr, es, ...)",
            "  narrator    default text-to-speech voice (type \"voice\")",
            "  comment     short description for the course card",
            "  logo        image for the course card",
            "  icon        replaces the default LiaScript icon",
            "  mode        Presentation | Slides | Textbook",
            "  repository  link to the source repository",
            "  edit        true or a URL to edit the course online",
            "  link        additional CSS files, one per line",
            "  script      additional JavaScript files, one per line",
            "  import      macros of other courses/templates",
            "  translation link to a translated version",
            "",
            "Type \"lia-macro-\" to find snippets for single header fields.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!--",
            "  author:   Your Name",
            "  email:    your@email.com",
            "  version:  0.1.0",
            "  language: en",
            "  narrator: US English Female",
            "",
            "  comment:  A short description of your course.",
            "            Multiline is also okay.",
            "",
            "  logo:     ./img/logo.png",
            "",
            "  link:     https://cdn.example.com/library.css",
            "  script:   https://cdn.example.com/library.js",
            "",
            "  translation: Deutsch  translations/German.md",
            "  -->",
            "",
            "  # Course Title",
            "",
            "The values can be referred to by the macros @author, @email, @version and @date."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-header-main"
    },
    "<!-- name: ... -->": {
        "body": "<!--\n${1:author:   Section Author}\n${2:narrator: UK English Female}\n-->",
        "description": [
            "A section header is an HTML comment directly after a heading. It overwrites settings of the main header for this section only, e.g. the author, the narrator voice, macros, additional scripts or styles (link, script), formula macros or persistent.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  # Course",
            "",
            "  ## Section",
            "  <!--",
            "  narrator: Australian Female",
            "  -->",
            "",
            "  This section is read with an Australian voice.",
            "",
            "  ### Sub Section",
            "  <!--",
            "  author:     Section Author",
            "  email:      section.author@email.com",
            "  persistent: true",
            "  -->"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-header-section"
    },
    "<!-- style ... --> ```` /--=... `````": {
        "body": "<!-- style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 315px;\" -->\n``````````````````````````````````````````````````\n${1:                             .--->  F\n    A       B     C   D     /\n    *-------*-----*---*----*----->  E\n             \\            ^ \\\n              v          /   '--->  G\n               B --> C -'}\n``````````````````````````````````````````````````",
        "description": [
            "Style an ASCII art drawing with an HTML comment directly above it, e.g. to center it, limit its width or change the colors of lines (stroke) and filled shapes (fill).",
            "",
            "Examples:",
            "",
            "  <!-- style=\"display: block;",
            "              margin-left: auto;",
            "              margin-right: auto;",
            "              max-width: 315px;\" -->",
            "  ``` ascii",
            "                           .--->  F",
            "      A       B     C   D     /",
            "      *-------*-----*---*----*----->  E",
            "               \\            ^ \\",
            "                v          /   '--->  G",
            "                 B --> C -'",
            "  ```"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-ascii-example-css"
    },
    "<!-- style=\"...\" --> block": {
        "body": "<!-- style=\"${1:color: purple; font-size: 1.2em;}\" ${2:class=\"${3:animated rollIn}\"} -->\n${4:This whole paragraph is styled.}",
        "description": [
            "Style any block (paragraph, list, table, quote, code, ...) by placing an HTML comment with attributes directly above it. Any HTML attribute works, e.g. style, class or id. The comment is invisible in other Markdown viewers.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- style=\"color: purple; font-size: 1.2em\" class=\"animated rollIn\" -->",
            "  This whole paragraph is styled.",
            "",
            "  <!-- style=\"width: 50%\" -->",
            "  | <!-- style=\"background: azure\" --> Header 1 | Header 2 |",
            "  |:--------------------------------------------|:---------|",
            "  | <!-- style=\"background: coral\" --> Item 1   | Item 2   |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-style-block"
    },
    "<!-- type=\"a\" --> 1. ...": {
        "body": "<!-- type=\"${1|a,A,i,I,1|}\" -->\n1. ${2:first}\n2. ${3:second}\n3. ${4:third}",
        "description": [
            "Change the numbering style of an ordered list with an attribute comment directly above it:",
            "",
            "  type=\"a\" / \"A\"   letters",
            "  type=\"i\" / \"I\"   roman numerals",
            "",
            "Any CSS list style works as well, e.g. <!-- style=\"list-style-type: lower-greek\" -->.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!-- type=\"i\" -->",
            "  1. first",
            "  2. second"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-list-type"
    },
    "<!--- ignored comment --->": {
        "body": "<!---\n${1:This note is ignored by LiaScript.}\n--->",
        "description": [
            "A comment that starts with three or more dashes (<!--- ... --->) is dropped completely by LiaScript. A normal HTML comment (<!-- ... -->) is read as attributes for the next element. Use it for notes to yourself or to comment out content.",
            "",
            "Important: always leave a blank line between this comment and any other HTML comment (e.g. a styling or chart comment). Two comments without a blank line are merged by the Markdown parser and the following element is shown as plain text.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!---",
            "  TODO: add a better picture here",
            "  --->",
            "",
            "  <!-- data-type=\"LinePlot\" -->",
            "  | x | y |",
            "  |---|---|",
            "  | 1 | 2 |"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-comment-ignore"
    },
    "<details><summary>...": {
        "body": "<details>\n\n<summary>${1:title}</summary>\n\n${2:body}\n\n</details>",
        "description": [
            "The details and summary tags are standard HTML tags and GitHub also supports their usage with Markdown. These tags offer a neat way to define something what is nowadays called accordion. Thus, your user can click on the summary text to make the body of the details-tag appear.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <details>",
            "",
            "  <summary>**Honest Textbook ads (click to enlarge)**</summary>",
            "",
            "  !?[If High School and College Textbooks Were Honest - Honest Ads](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lhSjYT7pWkw)",
            "",
            "  </details>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-details"
    },
    "<lia-chart option=\"...\">": {
        "body": "<lia-chart option=\"{\n  title: { text: '${1:Custom ECharts diagram}' },\n  xAxis: { type: 'value' },\n  yAxis: { type: 'value' },\n  series: [{ type: '${2:line}', data: ${3:[[0,0],[1,1],[2,4],[3,9]]} }]\n}\"></lia-chart>",
        "description": [
            "For diagrams that cannot be generated from a table, use the lia-chart element directly. The option attribute takes a complete Apache ECharts configuration. Scripts can also generate it dynamically: return \"HTML: <lia-chart option='...'></lia-chart>\".",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <lia-chart option=\"{",
            "    title: { text: 'Squares' },",
            "    xAxis: { type: 'value' },",
            "    yAxis: { type: 'value' },",
            "    series: [{ type: 'line', data: [[0,0],[1,1],[2,4],[3,9]] }]",
            "  }\"></lia-chart>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-chart"
    },
    "<lia-keep> ... </lia-keep>": {
        "body": "<lia-keep>\n${1:<table>\n  <tr><td rowspan=\"2\">raw</td><td>HTML</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>without Markdown parsing</td></tr>\n</table>}\n</lia-keep>",
        "description": [
            "Everything inside <lia-keep> is passed through as raw HTML: no Markdown parsing and no indentation checks. Useful for complex hand-written HTML, such as tables with rowspan or colspan.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <lia-keep>",
            "  <table>",
            "    <tr><td rowspan=\"2\">A</td><td>B</td></tr>",
            "    <tr><td>C</td></tr>",
            "  </table>",
            "  </lia-keep>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-keep"
    },
    "<script format=\"...\" locale=\"...\">": {
        "body": "<script format=\"${1|number,datetime,relativetime,list,pluralrules|}\" locale=\"${2:de-DE}\">\n${3:1234567.891}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "Formats the result of a script with the JavaScript Intl API for the given locale. Only the display changes, connected scripts still get the raw value.",
            "",
            "  format=\"number\"        1234.5 -> 1.234,5 (de-DE)",
            "  format=\"datetime\"      dates and times",
            "  format=\"relativetime\"  \"in 3 days\"",
            "  format=\"list\"          \"a, b and c\"",
            "  format=\"pluralrules\"   plural category",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script input=\"date\" value=\"2024-05-16\" format=\"datetime\" locale=\"de\">",
            "  \"@input\"",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-format"
    },
    "<script input=\"range\">": {
        "body": "<script input=\"range\" value=\"${1:0}\" min=\"${2:0}\" max=\"${3:360}\" step=\"${4:1}\">\n${5:let deg = @input\n\"sin(\" + deg + \"°) = \" + Math.sin(deg * Math.PI / 180).toFixed(3)}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "A slider that re-runs the script on every change. @input is replaced by the current number.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script input=\"range\" value=\"0\" min=\"0\" max=\"360\" step=\"1\">",
            "  let deg = @input",
            "  \"sin(\" + deg + \"°) = \" + Math.sin(deg * Math.PI / 180).toFixed(3)",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-input-range"
    },
    "<script input=\"select\" options=\"...\">": {
        "body": "<script input=\"${1|select,radio|}\" value=\"${2:red}\" options=\"${3:red|green|blue}\">\n${4:\"Selected color: @input\"}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "A dropdown (select) or a group of radio buttons. The options are separated by | in the options attribute, value sets the initial selection.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script input=\"radio\" value=\"1\" options=\"1|2|3|Hello World\">",
            "  \"Selected option: @input\"",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-input-select"
    },
    "<script input=\"submit\" default=\"...\">": {
        "body": "<script input=\"submit\" default=\"${1:roll the dice}\">\n${2:Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "A button that runs the script only when it is clicked. default sets the text that is shown before the first click. Use input=\"button\" instead to run it also once on load.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script input=\"submit\" default=\"roll the dice\">",
            "  Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-input-submit"
    },
    "<script input=\"type\">": {
        "body": "<script input=\"${1|text,number,range,checkbox,radio,select,button,submit,date,time,datetime-local,color,email,password,search,tel,url,week,month|}\" value=\"${2:Hello World}\">\n${3:let str = \"@input\"\nstr.split(\"\").reverse().join(\"\")}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "Turns a script into a small interactive widget. input=\"...\" selects an HTML5 input type, value=\"...\" sets the initial value, and @input is replaced by the current value before the script runs. The result is shown next to the input.",
            "",
            "text, number, range, radio, select and checkbox run on every change. button runs on load and on every click. submit only runs when clicked. The other types (date, time, color, ...) run when the field loses focus.",
            "",
            "Extra attributes: min, max, step, options=\"a|b|c\" (radio/select), default=\"...\" (text shown before the first run of button/submit).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script input=\"number\" value=\"1\" min=\"0\" max=\"100\">",
            "  let i = @input",
            "  \"Square of \" + i + \" = \" + i * i",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-input"
    },
    "<script output=\"name\"> ... @input(`name`)": {
        "body": "<script input=\"${1:number}\" value=\"${2:2}\" output=\"${3:a}\">\n@input\n</script>\n${4:squared is}\n<script>\n${5:@input(`a`) ** 2}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "Connect scripts: a script with output=\"name\" publishes its current result. Other scripts on the same slide can read it with @input(`name`) (with backticks) and re-run automatically when it changes.",
            "",
            "If two scripts depend on each other, give both a default=\"...\" value. Otherwise the first calculation fails.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script input=\"checkbox\" value=\"true\" output=\"P\" default=\"true\">",
            "  @input",
            "  </script>",
            "  AND",
            "  <script input=\"checkbox\" value=\"false\" output=\"Q\" default=\"false\">",
            "  @input",
            "  </script>",
            "  =",
            "  <script>",
            "  @input(`P`) && @input(`Q`)",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-connect"
    },
    "<script> \"LIA: terminal\" + send.handle </script>": {
        "body": "```${1:js}\n${2:// type JavaScript into the terminal after executing this block}\n```\n<script>\n  send.handle(\"input\", (input) => {\n    ${3:send.lia(String(eval(input)))}\n  })\n  send.handle(\"stop\", (e) => {\n    ${4:console.log(\"stopped\")}\n  })\n  \"LIA: terminal\"\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "An interactive terminal (REPL): ending the script with \"LIA: terminal\" opens an input line below the output after execution. send.handle(\"input\", callback) is called for every line the user sends, send.handle(\"stop\", callback) when the user stops the execution.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  ```js",
            "  // execute me and type 2+2 into the terminal",
            "  ```",
            "  <script>",
            "    send.handle(\"input\", (input) => send.lia(String(eval(input))))",
            "    \"LIA: terminal\"",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-code-terminal"
    },
    "<script> /* quiz */ </script>": {
        "body": "<script>\n  ${1:// @input is replaced by the current quiz input:\n  // * text / gap text  -> raw text, use it as `@input`\n  // * single choice    -> number (-1 if nothing selected)\n  // * selection        -> number of the selected option\n  // * multiple choice  -> array, e.g. [0,1,1] (1 = checked)\n  // * matrix           -> nested array, one entry per row\n  let input = @input;\n\n  true; // return true if solved, otherwise false}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "A generic script that can be attached directly after any quiz. Before it runs, @input is replaced as text, so its form depends on the quiz type:",
            "",
            "  text / gap text  -> raw text, wrap it in backticks: `@input`",
            "  single choice    -> number (-1 if nothing is selected)",
            "  selection        -> number of the selected option",
            "  multiple choice  -> array, e.g. [0,1,1] (1 = checked)",
            "  matrix           -> nested array, e.g. [[1,0,0], 1]",
            "",
            "The last expression has to be true (solved) or false (not solved).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "      [[52]]",
            "      <script>",
            "        Number(`@input`) === 52",
            "      </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-js"
    },
    "<script> /*single choice*/ </script>": {
        "body": "<script>\n  // @input is replaced by the index of the selected option\n  // (0 = first option, -1 = nothing selected)\n  let selected = @input;\n\n  ${1:selected === 1}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "A script after a single-choice quiz can change how it is checked, e.g. to accept other answers or to count trials. @input is replaced by a number (unquoted):",
            "",
            "  -1 -> nothing selected",
            "   0 -> the first option",
            "   1 -> the second option ...",
            "",
            "The last expression has to be true (solved) or false (not solved).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "      [( )] Check this ...",
            "      [(X)] ... or this",
            "      <script>",
            "        let selected = @input;",
            "        selected === 0 || selected === 1",
            "      </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-single-js"
    },
    "<script> /*single multiple*/ </script>": {
        "body": "<script>\n  // @input is replaced by an array such as [0,1,0,1],\n  // with 1 for a checked and 0 for an unchecked box\n  let checked = @input;\n\n  ${1:// accept every answer with at least one correct option\n  checked[1] === 1 || checked[3] === 1}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "A script after a multiple-choice quiz can change how it is checked, e.g. to let partially correct answers pass. @input is replaced by an array (unquoted) with one entry per option:",
            "",
            "  1 -> checked",
            "  0 -> unchecked",
            "",
            "The last expression has to be true (solved) or false (not solved).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  Get at least one right ...",
            "",
            "      [[ ]] Wrong.",
            "      [[X]] Right.",
            "      [[ ]] Wrong.",
            "      [[X]] Right.",
            "      <script>",
            "        let checked = @input;",
            "        (checked[1] === 1 || checked[3] === 1) &&",
            "        checked[0] === 0 && checked[2] === 0",
            "      </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-multiple-js"
    },
    "<script> async ... \"LIA: wait\" </script>": {
        "body": "```${1:js}\n${2:// asynchronous code\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\"done\"), 1000)}\n```\n<script>\n  ${3:eval(`@input`)\n  setTimeout(() => send.lia(\"LIA: stop\"), 2000)}\n  \"LIA: wait\"\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "Executable code with asynchronous results. End the script with \"LIA: wait\", so that LiaScript keeps the code block in the running state. Send output later with send.lia(...) or console.log(...) and finish with send.lia(\"LIA: stop\").",
            "",
            "Useful control strings and functions:",
            "  \"LIA: wait\"                  keep running (async)",
            "  \"LIA: stop\"                  finish the execution",
            "  \"LIA: clear\"                 clear the terminal",
            "  send.lia(\"text\")             print output and keep running",
            "  send.lia(\"text\", [], true)   print as success",
            "  send.lia(\"text\", [], false)  print as error",
            "  send.liascript(\"## md\")      render LiaScript in the output",
            "  send.lia(\"HTML: <b>x</b>\")   render raw HTML in the output",
            "  console.log/warn/error/debug(...)",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  ```js",
            "  let i = 0",
            "  const id = setInterval(() => console.log(i++), 500)",
            "  setTimeout(() => { clearInterval(id); send.lia(\"LIA: stop\") }, 3000)",
            "  ```",
            "  <script>",
            "    eval(`@input`)",
            "    \"LIA: wait\"",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-code-async"
    },
    "<script> send.output ... \"LIA: wait\" </script>": {
        "body": "<script>\nlet i = 0\nsetInterval(function() {\n  ${1:i++\n  send.output(\"counting \" + i)}\n}, ${2:1000})\n\"LIA: wait\"\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "A self-updating script component: return \"LIA: wait\" (or call send.wait()) to keep it running, then call send.output(...) for every update. The process keeps running in the background when the user switches slides, until send.stop() is called.",
            "",
            "send.output only works in standalone scripts, not in scripts of code blocks (use send.lia there).",
            "",
            "Shortcuts: send.wait(), send.stop(), send.clear()",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script>",
            "  setInterval(() => send.output(new Date().toLocaleTimeString()), 1000)",
            "  \"LIA: wait\"",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-counter"
    },
    "<script> window.x = ... </script>": {
        "body": "<script>\nwindow.${1:shared} = ${2:22 ** 3}\n</script>\n<script>\nwindow.${1:shared} / 2\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "Every script runs in its own local scope, so variables of one script are not visible in another. To share values between scripts, attach them to window explicitly. For live updates between scripts, prefer output=\"...\" and @input(`...`) (see lia-script-connect).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script> window.a = 22 ** 3 </script>",
            "  <script> window.a / 2 </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-window"
    },
    "<script>...</script> inline": {
        "body": "<script>${1:Math.sin(Math.PI / 2)}</script>",
        "description": [
            "A <script> tag in the text (without a code block) is a JavaScript component. It runs automatically when the slide is shown, and the value of its last expression is displayed in place, like an inline element. If the result is undefined, nothing is shown.",
            "",
            "Every script has its own scope. To share values between scripts, attach them to window. Scripts can be combined with animations ({1}{<script>...</script>}) to run them at a certain step.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  $ \\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) = $ <script>Math.sin(Math.PI / 2)</script>",
            "",
            "  Today is <script>new Date().toLocaleDateString()</script>."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-inline"
    },
    "<script>...@input...</script>": {
        "body": "<script>\n  try{\n    eval(`@input`);\n  } catch (e) {\n    var log = e.stack.match(/((.*?):(.*))\\\\n.*?(:(\\\\d+):(\\\\d+)\\\\)\\\\n)/);\n    var err_msg = new LiaError(log[1] + \" =>  (\" + log[4], 1);\n    err_msg.add_detail(0, log[3], \"error\", log[5]-1, log[6]);\n    throw err_msg;\n  }\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "This is an extended executable version of a code block, it shows how errors can be extracted and fed back by using LiaError to show inline errors, warnings, or information.",
            "If you are using a project and want to integrate more than one file into your execution, you will have to use the parameterized @input(int) macro, the integer is defined by the file order (@input defaults to 0).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  <script>",
            "    try{",
            "      eval(`@input`); // code to be evaluated",
            "    } catch (e) {",
            "      // do some pattern matching to get the error string, the line number and column",
            "      var log = e.stack.match(/((.*?):(.*))\\n.*?(:(\\d+):(\\d+)\\)\\n)/);",
            "",
            "      // create a new LiaError object with",
            "      // param1: an error message string",
            "      // param2: add the number of files involved as int",
            "      var err_msg = new LiaError(log[1] + \" =>  (\" + log[4], 1);",
            "",
            "      // add as many information to your error message",
            "      // param1: file id",
            "      // param2: additional information string",
            "      // param3: type, either \"error\", \"info\", or \"warning\"",
            "      // param4: line number",
            "      // param5: column number",
            "      err_msg.add_detail(0, log[3], \"error\", log[5]-1, log[6]);",
            "",
            "      throw err_msg;   // finally just throw it away",
            "    }",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-code-js"
    },
    "<section> ... </section>": {
        "body": "<section>\n\n${1:## Grouped Sub-Heading}\n\n${2:Content that stays on this slide.}\n\n</section>",
        "description": [
            "Every heading (#, ##, ###, ...) starts a new slide. To use headings inside a slide without creating new slides, wrap them in a <section> or <article> tag. Leave blank lines around the content, so that it is parsed as Markdown.",
            "",
