    "@name ... body ... @end": {
        "body": "@${1:name}\n${2:param1 -> __@0__}\n@end",
        "description": [
            "Use macros to deal with repetitive task and to make the document more readable. Multi-line macros start with an (@your_name) and end with (@end). Parameters can also be passed as to single line macros.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  ## section",
            "  <!--",
            "  @evalJS",
            "  <script>",
            "    try{",
            "      eval(`@input`);",
            "    } catch (e) {",
            "      var log = e.stack.match(/((.*?):(.*))",
            ".*?(:(d+):(d+))",
            ")/);",
            "      var err_msg = new LiaError(log[1] + \" =>  (\" + log[4], 1);",
            "      err_msg.add_detail(0, log[3], \"error\", log[5]-1, log[6]);",
            "      throw err_msg;",
            "    }",
            "  </script>",
            "  @end",
            "  -->",
            "",
            "  eval this code with erros:",
            "",
            "  ```js",
            "  let x = 12;",
            "  x * c;",
            "  ```",
            "  @evalJS"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-definition-block"
    },
    "@name: one line": {
        "body": "@${1:name}: ${2:line}",
        "description": [
            "Use macros to deal with repetitive task and to make the document more readable. You can define your own macros within the main and section headers. Start your macro with an (@) sign and give it a name. Separate the name from the body with a colon (:). If you want to parameterize your macro, use @0, @1, ... for the parameter id. It is a simple string substitution mechanism, and the parameters are separated by a comma (,). If you have commas in your parameters, then use back-tics (`) to enclose this parameter. And triple back-tics (```) to enclose multi-line parameters.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  ## section",
            "  <!--",
            "  @red: <!-- style=\"color: red;\" -->",
            "",
            "  @bold_italic: __@0__ _@1_",
            "  -->",
            "",
            "  @red",
            "  This whole paragraph will be read and this will be:",
            "  @bold_italic(I am Bold,`I am Italic,, I am too`)."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-definition-line"
    },
    "@onload ... @end": {
        "body": "@onload\n${1:window.myGlobal = 42}\n@end",
        "description": [
            "Header block: JavaScript that is executed once when the course is loaded, e.g. to configure a library or to define global helper functions. It is also imported by other courses that import this one.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!--",
            "  script: https://cdn.example.com/library.js",
            "",
            "  @onload",
            "  window.greet = (name) => \"Hello \" + name",
            "  @end",
            "  -->",
            "",
            "  <script>greet(\"World\")</script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-onload"
    },
    "@persistent": {
        "body": "persistent: ${1|true,false|}",
        "description": [
            "persistent can be used in two places:",
            "",
            "In a section header, persistent: true keeps the section alive when the user navigates to another slide. Scripts, widgets and animations are not re-created when the user comes back. This is also needed if scripts on other slides should be able to send events to this section.",
            "",
            "In the main header, persistent: false prevents that anything of this course (states and the entry in the local course index) is stored in the browser.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  ## Live Clock",
            "  <!--",
            "  persistent: true",
            "  -->",
            "",
            "  <script>",
            "  setInterval(() => send.output(new Date().toLocaleTimeString()), 1000)",
            "  \"LIA: wait\"",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-persistent"
    },
    "@section": {
        "body": "@section",
        "description": [
            "Gets replaced by the current section number."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-section"
    },
    "@style ... @end": {
        "body": "@style\n${1:.highlight {\n  color: crimson;\n  font-weight: bold;\n\\}}\n@end",
        "description": [
            "Header block: CSS rules for the whole course. Combine it with class attributes to style single elements.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <!--",
            "  @style",
            "  .highlight {",
            "    color: crimson;",
            "    font-weight: bold;",
            "  }",
            "  @end",
            "  -->",
            "",
            "  This is **important**<!-- class=\"highlight\" -->."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-style"
    },
    "@uid": {
        "body": "@uid",
        "description": [
            "Generates a UID that can be used to name tags uniquely."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-uid"
    },
    "@version": {
        "body": "@version",
        "description": [
            "Gets replaced by the version defined in the header."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-macro-version"
    },
    "LIA.classroom publish/subscribe": {
        "body": "<script>\nif (LIA.classroom.connected) {\n  const id = LIA.classroom.subscribe(\"${1:topic}\", (message) => {\n    ${2:send.output(JSON.stringify(message))}\n  })\n  LIA.classroom.publish(\"${1:topic}\", ${3:{ hello: \"world\" \\}})\n}\n\"LIA: wait\"\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "LIA.classroom lets scripts exchange messages with all participants of a live classroom session (peer-to-peer). It is only active while a classroom is connected.",
            "",
            "  LIA.classroom.connected                 true if a session is active",
            "  LIA.classroom.on(\"connect\", cb)         also \"disconnect\"",
            "  LIA.classroom.publish(\"topic\", data)    send to all peers",
            "  LIA.classroom.subscribe(\"topic\", cb)    returns an id",
            "  LIA.classroom.unsubscribe(id)",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  <script>",
            "  LIA.classroom.on(\"connect\", () => send.output(\"connected\"))",
            "  \"LIA: wait\"",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-script-classroom"
    },
    "Title ===== (setext)": {
        "body": "${1:Local Subsection}\n${2:====================}",
        "description": [
            "Setext-style headings (text underlined with === or ---) do not start a new slide and do not appear in the table of contents. Use them for local structure within a slide.",
            "",
            "  ===   -> large local heading",
            "  ---   -> smaller local heading",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  ## Section Title",
            "",
            "  Local Subsection",
            "  ================",
            "",
            "  Local Sub-Subsection",
            "  --------------------"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-title-setext"
    },
    "[( |X)] text": {
        "body": "[(${1: })] ${2:enter your text}",
        "description": [
            "Single-choice quiz (radio buttons): one line per option. [(X)] or [(x)] marks a correct option, [( )] a wrong one. If more than one option is marked, the user has to select only one of them. A starting dash (- [( )] ...) is allowed.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  This is a single choice quiz with three options:",
            "",
            "      [( )] Add as many lines as you want.",
            "      [(X)] The X marks the correct answer!",
            "      [( )] This one is wrong."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-single-choice"
    },
    "[(1)(2)(0)]": {
        "body": "[(${1:0})(${2:1})(${3:2})]\n[         ] ${4:text}\n[         ] ${5:text}\n[         ] ${6:text}",
        "description": [
            "Single-choice matrix survey: the header contains the column identifiers in parentheses, every following row ([ ] + text) becomes its own single-choice question. Identifiers can be numbers or words.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  Give marks:",
            "",
            "      [(-1)(0)(1)]",
            "      [          ] LiaScript is great?",
            "      [          ] I would use it to make online **courses**?",
            "      [          ] I would use it for online **surveys**?",
            "",
            "  What is your opinion:",
            "",
            "      [(agree)(unsure)(maybe not so)]",
            "      [                             ] LiaScript is great?",
            "      [                             ] I would use it to make online **courses**?",
            "      [                             ] I would use it for online **surveys**?"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-survey-single-choice-matrix"
    },
    "[(1)]...[(:...)]...": {
        "body": "[(${1:1})] ${2:option 1}\n[(${3:2})] ${4:option 2}\n[(${5:3})] ${6:option 3}",
        "description": [
            "Single-choice survey: like a single-choice quiz, but the parentheses contain an identifier instead of an X. Numeric identifiers let the classroom view plot the results as a distribution, while text identifiers are shown as categories. Identifiers do not have to be ordered or sequential.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  Choose between different options:",
            "",
            "    [(1)] option 1",
            "    [(2)] option 2",
            "    [(0)] option 3",
            "",
            "  What is your favorite color:",
            "",
            "    [(red)]   red?",
            "    [(blue)]  blue?",
            "    [(green)] green?"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-survey-single-choice"
    },
    "[[ |X][ |X]...] text": {
        "body": "[[${1:column 1}] [${2:column 2}] [${3:column 3}]]\n[    [${4:X}]          [${5: }]          [${6: }]     ] ${7:multiple-choice row}\n[    (${8: })          (${9:X})          (${10: })     ] ${11:single-choice row}",
        "description": [
            "Matrix quiz: the first line holds the column labels. Every following row starts with [ and ends with ] followed by the row text. The cells of a row define its type: [ ] / [X] cells make a multiple-choice row, ( ) / (X) cells a single-choice row. All cells of one row must use the same type. Spacing is only cosmetic.",
            "",
            "The brackets in the header are only labels. Use [...] or (...) so that you can put the other type of bracket into a label as text.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  - [[male (der)] (female [die]) [neuter (das)]]",
            "  - [    [X]           [ ]             [ ]     ]  Mann - German for man",
            "  - [    ( )           (X)             ( )     ]  Frau - German for woman"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-matrix-choice"
    },
    "[[ |X]] text": {
        "body": "[[${1: }]] ${2:enter your text}",
        "description": [
            "Multiple-choice quiz (checkboxes): one line per option. [[X]] or [[x]] marks an option that has to be checked, [[ ]] one that has to stay unchecked. A starting dash (- [[ ]] ...) is allowed.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  This is a multiple choice quiz with four options:",
            "",
            "      [[ ]] Add as many lines as you want.",
            "      [[X]] The X marks the options that have to be checked!",
            "      [[X]] There can be several of them.",
            "      [[ ]] Empty brackets have to stay unchecked."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-multiple-choice"
    },
    "[[!]] <script> ... </script>": {
        "body": "[[!]]\n<script>\n  ${1:// check anything you want and return true or false\n  true;}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "Generic quiz: [[!]] has no input element of its own. The quiz is solved if the script evaluates to true and not solved if it evaluates to false. Use it for checks that do not fit the other quiz types, e.g. to test the state of an interactive element or the result of a code block.",
            "",
            "Use alert(...) to inspect values while developing.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  Only lucky people can solve this quiz:",
            "",
            "      [[!]]",
            "      <script>",
            "        Math.random() < 0.2",
            "      </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-generic-js"
    },
    "[[1][2][0]]": {
        "body": "[[${1:0}][${2:1}][${3:2}]]\n[         ] ${4:text}\n[         ] ${5:text}\n[         ] ${6:text}",
        "description": [
            "Multiple-choice matrix survey: the header contains the column identifiers in brackets, every following row ([ ] + text) becomes its own multiple-choice question. Identifiers can be numbers or words.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  Give marks:",
            "",
            "      [[-1][0][1]]",
            "      [          ] LiaScript is great?",
            "      [          ] I would use it to make online **courses**?",
            "      [          ] I would use it for online **surveys**?",
            "",
            "  What is your opinion:",
            "",
            "      [[agree][unsure][maybe not so]]",
            "      [                             ] LiaScript is great?",
            "      [                             ] I would use it to make online **courses**?",
            "      [                             ] I would use it for online **surveys**?"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-survey-multiple-choice-matrix"
    },
    "[[1]]...[[:...]]...": {
        "body": "[[${1:1}]] ${2:option 1}\n[[${3:2}]] ${4:option 2}\n[[${5:3}]] ${6:option 3}",
        "description": [
            "Multiple-choice survey: like a multiple-choice quiz, but the brackets contain an identifier instead of an X. Start an identifier with a number (e.g. [[1 red]]) to get a numeric instead of a categorical representation in the classroom view.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  Choose as many options as your want:",
            "",
            "    [[1]] option 1",
            "    [[2]] option 2",
            "    [[0]] option 0",
            "",
            "  Mark all colors you like:",
            "",
            "    [[red]]   red?",
            "    [[blue]]  blue?",
            "    [[green]] green?",
            "    [[none]]  None of these ..."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-survey-multiple-choice"
    },
    "[[?]] hint": {
        "body": "[[?]] ${1:enter your hint}",
        "description": [
            "Hints can be added directly after any type of quiz, as many as you want. They are revealed one at a time when the user clicks the hint button. A starting dash (- [[?]] ...) is allowed.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  A text quiz with two hints:",
            "",
            "      [[solution]]",
            "      [[?]] This is the first hint",
            "      [[?]] This is a second hint",
            "",
            "  And a multiple choice quiz with one hint:",
            "",
            "      [[X]] This is correct!",
            "      [[ ]] This is wrong!",
            "      [[?]] Click on the first option!"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-hint"
    },
    "[[___ ___]]": {
        "body": "[[___ ___ ___]]",
        "description": [
            "A multi-line text survey (textarea). Every group of underscores separated by a space adds one row. The number of groups sets the number of rows, the length of a group does not matter. One pair of double brackets always creates exactly one input field. For several fields, write several surveys, each with its own question above it.",
            "",
            "In a live classroom session, the answers of a textarea are listed one below the other, which suits longer answers.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  A two-line text survey:",
            "",
            "      [[___ ___]]",
            "",
            "  Please describe your opinion in a few sentences:",
            "",
            "      [[___ ___ ___ ___ ___ ___]]"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-survey-text"
    },
    "[[___]]": {
        "body": "[[___]]",
        "description": [
            "Surveys use the quiz brackets but have no correct answer, only a submit button. Three or more underscores in double brackets create a one-line text input.",
            "",
            "In a live classroom session, the answers of one-line inputs are shown as a word cloud, where comma-separated terms are counted. Use it for keywords or short phrases.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  Which programming languages do you know? Separate them with commas.",
            "",
            "      [[___]]"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-survey-text-line"
    },
    "[[select|_wrong_|(right)]]": {
        "body": "[[ ${1:option} | (${2:correct option}) | ${3:option} ]]",
        "description": [
            "Selection quiz: a dropdown with options separated by `|`. The correct option is put into parentheses, and more than one option can be correct. Options may contain Markdown, images or formulas. Selections do not support a starting dash.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  What is the color of the sky?",
            "",
            "      [[ red | (__blue__) | green | (black at night) ]]",
            "",
            "  What is the derivative of $f(x) = x^6$?",
            "",
            "      [[ $f'(x) = 6$ | ( $f'(x) = 6x^5$ ) | $f'(x) = 5x^6$ ]]"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-select"
    },
    "[[solution]]": {
        "body": "[[${1:solution}]]",
        "description": [
            "Text quiz: put the solution into double brackets and the user gets an input field. The answer is compared with the solution string. Text quizzes do not support a starting dash.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  What is the color of the sky?",
            "",
            "      [[blue]]",
            "",
            "If several answers should be accepted, add a script (lia-quiz-text-js) or use a selection (lia-quiz-select)."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-text"
    },
    "[[solution]]<script> ... </script>": {
        "body": "[[${1:solution}]]\n<script>\n  ${2:// @input is replaced by the raw user input, so wrap it in backticks\n  let input = `@input`.trim().toLowerCase();\n  input === \"solution\" || input === \"answer\";}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "Text quiz with a custom check. The script is placed directly after the quiz. Before it runs, @input is replaced by the raw text the user typed, so wrap it in backticks (`@input`) to get a JavaScript string. The last expression of the script has to be true (solved) or false (not solved).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  What is the color of the sky?",
            "",
            "      [[blue]]",
            "      <script>",
            "        let input = `@input`.trim().toLowerCase();",
            "        input === \"blue\" || input === \"azure\";",
            "      </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-quiz-text-js"
    },
    "[^ref]": {
        "body": "[^${1:ref}]",
        "description": [
            "Multiline footnotes have to be split into a reference and into a comment. Footnote references are defined by brackets with a starting caret and can appear everywhere within the text. Footnote texts have to be put at the end of the same section, because LiaScript parses every slide on its own. The id can be a number, a word or even an emoji.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "Here is a footnote reference, [^1] and another.[^longnote]",
            "",
            "...",
            "",
            "[^1]: Here is the footnote text.",
            "",
            "[^longnote]: Here's one with multiple blocks.",
            "",
            "               Subsequent paragraphs are indented to show that they",
            "               belong to the previous footnote."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-footnote-reference"
    },
    "[^ref](text)": {
        "body": "[^${1:ref}](${2:explanation})",
        "description": [
            "Inline Footnotes are composed of a reference in brackets with a starting caret. The following text has to be put into parentheses, only one line is allowed.",
            "",
            "   Example with [^1](inline _footnote_) and some more text."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-footnote-inline"
    },
    "[^ref]: some comments ...": {
        "body": "[^${1:ref}]: ${2:some comments}",
        "description": [
            "Multiline footnotes have to be split into a reference and into a comment. Footnote texts start with a reference \"tag\", which is followed by a colon. They have to be put into the end of each section otherwise they are not interpreted as footnote texts and multi-block footnotes have to follow the previous indentation.",
            "Example:",
            "...",
            "",
            "[^1]: Here is the footnote text.",
            "",
            "[^longnote]: Here's one with multiple blocks.",
            "",
            "               Subsequent paragraphs are indented to show that they",
            "               belong to the previous footnote."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-footnote-text"
    },
    "[name](mailto:...)": {
        "body": "[${1:Write me}](${2|mailto,tel|}:${3:you@example.com})",
        "description": [
            "Links with mailto: open the email program, links with tel: start a phone call on mobile devices.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  [Write me](mailto:you@example.com)",
            "",
            "  [Call me](tel:+491234567890)"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-mailto"
    },
    "[name](url \"info\")": {
        "body": "[${1:name}](${2:url} \"${3:info}\")",
        "description": [
            "An optional information can be attached to every link by adding double quotes after the link in the parentheses.",
            "",
            "Examples:",
            "  [LiaScript](https://liascript.github.io \"click Me\")",
            "",
            "  Local navigation:",
            "    [top](#1 \"by slide number\")",
            "    [top](#and-the-title-is \"by slide title\")"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-link-info"
    },
    "[name](url)": {
        "body": "[${1:name}](${2:url})",
        "description": [
            "Links can be added in many ways to a document (relative or absolute), either in common Markdown style by enclosed brackets ([]) and parentheses (()), or directly if it starts with http or www and matches the common url pattern.",
            "If you want to navigate within the current document, use hash tag and the number of the slide.",
            "And you can add additional html and css options by adding a trailing comment.",
            "",
            "Examples:",
            "  [LiaScript](https://liascript.github.io)",
            "",
            "  Also an accepted link: https://liascript.github.io",
            "",
            "  Local navigation by slide number: [top](#1)",
            "",
            "  Local navigation by slide title: [top](#some-title)",
            "",
            "  Autolink, no escaping of parentheses needed: <https://example.com/path_(with_parens)>",
            "",
            "  With styling [LiaScript](https://liascript.github.io)<!--",
            "    title = \"click me\"",
            "    style = \"color: red;\"",
            "  -->"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-link"
    },
    "[preview-lia](course-url)": {
        "body": "[preview-lia](${1:https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md})",
        "description": [
            "A link with the text preview-lia to the raw URL of another LiaScript course is rendered as a preview card with the title, description, logo and author taken from the course header. Clicking the card opens the course.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  [preview-lia](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/liaScript/docs/master/README.md)"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-preview-lia"
    },
    "[qr-code](url)": {
        "body": "[qr-code](${1:https://liascript.github.io})",
        "description": [
            "A link with the text qr-code is rendered as a QR code for its URL, e.g. so that students in a lecture can open a link on their phones. Other Markdown viewers show a normal link.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  [qr-code](https://liascript.github.io)"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-qr-code"
    },
    "^text^": {
        "body": "^${1:superscript}^",
        "description": [
            "Superscript text, enclosed by carets. This is a LiaScript extension. For subscripts or more complex notation use a formula ($ x_i $).",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  E = mc^2^ and the 1^st^ of May."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-text-superscript"
    },
    "___text___": {
        "body": "___${1:italic bold}___",
        "description": [
            "Bold and italic text, enclosed by triple underscores. Equivalent to ***text***.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  This is ___bold and italic___."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-text-italic-bold"
    },
    "__text__": {
        "body": "__${1:bold}__",
        "description": [
            "Bold text, enclosed by double underscores. Equivalent to **text**.",
            "",
            "Every emphasis span has to stay on one physical line.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  This is __bold__ and __bold with _italic_ inside__."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-text-bold"
    },
    "_text_": {
        "body": "_${1:italic}_",
        "description": [
            "Italic text, enclosed by single underscores. Equivalent to *text*.",
            "",
            "Every emphasis span has to stay on one physical line. If the closing marker lands on the next line, stray markers will show up in the rendered text.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "  This is _italic_ and this is _also **bold** inside_."
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-text-italic"
    },
    "``` ... ``` ``` ... ``` ...": {
        "body": "``` ${1:js}     ${2:-EvalScript.js}\n${3:let who = data.first_name + \" \" + data.last_name;\n\nif(data.online) {\n  who + \" is online\"; \\}\nelse {\n  who + \" is NOT online\"; \\}}\n```\n``` ${4:json}    ${5:+Data.json}\n${6:{\n  \"first_name\" :  \"Sammy\",\n  \"last_name\"  :  \"Shark\",\n  \"online\"     :  true\n\\}}\n```\n<script>\n  ${7:// insert the JSON dataset into the local variable data\n  let data = @input(1);\n\n  // eval the script that uses this dataset\n  eval(`@input(0)`);}\n</script>",
        "description": [
            "If you want to create a project and thus separate data and code into multiple files, simply write multiple code blocks in a sequence. To make them executable as a whole, simply add a script tag to the end and use the parameterized @input(id) macro to compose your project properly. The file id is defined by the order of code blocks.",
            "See the link below to see more examples.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
            "  ``` js     -EvalScript.js",
            "  let who = data.first_name + \" \" + data.last_name;",
            "",
            "  if(data.online) { who + \" is online\"; }",
            "  else            { who + \" is NOT online\"; }",
            "  ```",
            "  ``` json    +Data.json",
            "  { \"first_name\" :  \"Sammy\",",
            "    \"last_name\"  :  \"Shark\",",
            "    \"online\"     :  true   }",
            "  ```",
            "  <script>",
            "    // insert the JSON dataset into the local variable data",
            "    let data = @input(1);",
            "",
            "    // eval the script that uses this dataset",
            "    eval(`@input(0)`);",
            "  </script>"
        ],
        "prefix": "lia-code-project"
    },
    "``` @[macro](url-param) ```": {
        "body": "@[${1:macro}](${2:url-param})",
        "description": [
            "This type of macro-call comes handy, if you want to refere to a certain URL and want other Markdown readers to show this information as a link. It is also possible to add additional parameters within parentheses, whereby the URL is always used as the last parameter.",
            "",
            "Example:",
            "",
